  "business_insights": [
    "Demand is geographically concentrated: Australia alone accounts for 12,683 of 49,208 transactions (25.8%), and the top 3 source countries together cover a large share of volume \u2014 marketing/localization spend is better targeted at a short list of countries than spread evenly.",
    "Attraction demand is highly concentrated too: Sacred Monkey Forest Sanctuary alone drew 12,369 visits (25.1% of all transactions) out of only 30 attractions in the catalogue \u2014 capacity planning and on-site experience investment should prioritize this small set of marquee sites.",
    "'Couples' is the dominant travel mode (41.3% of visits); Business travel is a minor segment (1.1%) but rates experiences highest on average (4.31/5) \u2014 a small, low-volume but high-satisfaction segment worth a dedicated offering rather than more inventory.",
    "2020\u20132021 volume collapsed to just 1.1% of total transactions (COVID-19 travel disruption is clearly visible in the data) with a slow partial recovery by 2022 \u2014 any trend/seasonality model trained on this data should treat 2020\u20132021 as an anomalous period rather than representative seasonality.",
    "Satisfaction varies materially by attraction type: 'Water Parks' rates highest on average (4.65/5) while 'Historic Sites' rates lowest (3.50/5) and also shows one of the higher shares of 1\u20132\u2605 ratings (16.8%) \u2014 a natural target for a service-quality review.",
    "Popularity and average rating show no meaningful correlation across attractions (r=0.12) \u2014 the most-visited sites are not simply the best-reviewed ones, so recommendation/ranking logic should weight both signals rather than defaulting to raw popularity.",
    "27.7% of the 33,530 unique travelers in the data made more than one visit (median 1 trip, max 26) \u2014 the base is dominated by one-time visitors, which matters for how much a collaborative-filtering recommender can rely on a user's own history versus needing strong cold-start handling (already implemented upstream in regression_features.csv)."
  ],
